# MMdotcom

Personal portfolio site for [Michael Matonis](https://michaelmatonis.com) — a Cleveland-based web developer with a background in Salesforce development. The site showcases skills, projects, downloadable resumes, and a contact form.

## Tech Stack

| Layer | Technologies |
|-------|--------------|
| **Frontend** | HTML, CSS, JavaScript (ES modules), native Web Components |
| **UI framework** | [MiComponents](https://github.com/matonism/micomponent) — a custom web component framework built on shadow DOM and a dynamic component loader |
| **Polyfills** | `@webcomponents/custom-elements`, `@webcomponents/webcomponentsjs` |
| **Local dev server** | [Express](https://expressjs.com/) |
| **Contact form backend** | AWS Lambda, API Gateway, [Nodemailer](https://nodemailer.com/) (Gmail SMTP) |
| **Hosting & CDN** | AWS S3, CloudFront, Route 53 |

## Project Structure

```
MMdotcom/
├── public/                      # Static site — deployed to S3
│   ├── index.html               # Site entry point
│   ├── pages/                   # Routed pages (home, contact, etc.)
│   ├── micomponents/            # Custom web components (PageHeader, ContactForm, …)
│   ├── micomponent-framework/   # MiCoolComponent base class and WebComponentLoader
│   └── utilities/               # Shared CSS, JS, images, and downloadable files
├── server/                      # Express dev server
├── lambda/                      # Contact-form email handler (AWS Lambda)
└── package.json
```

## Local Development

### Prerequisites

- [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) (LTS recommended)
- npm

### Setup

```bash
npm install
```

### Run

```bash
npm start
```

The Express server starts on **port 3000** (or the value of `PORT`) and serves the `public/` directory. Open [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000) in your browser.

The dev server also handles `/pages/*` routing so that directory-style URLs resolve to the correct HTML files.

## Deployment

Production hosting is on **AWS S3** fronted by **CloudFront**. Deployments require the [AWS CLI](https://aws.amazon.com/cli/) configured with credentials that can write to the S3 bucket and create CloudFront invalidations.

```bash
npm run deploy
```

This runs two steps:

1. **`deploy:sync`** — syncs `./public` to `s3://michaelmatonis.com/`
2. **`deploy:invalidate`** — creates a CloudFront cache invalidation for `/*` so changes appear immediately

You can also run the steps individually:

```bash
npm run deploy:sync
npm run deploy:invalidate
```

### Lambda (contact form)

The contact form posts to an API Gateway endpoint backed by the function in `lambda/`. After deploying a new Lambda version, you may need to visit [Google's display-unlock-captcha page](https://accounts.google.com/b/0/displayunlockcaptcha) before Gmail will accept SMTP connections from the new deployment.

Lambda environment variables:

| Variable | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `email_address` | Gmail address used to send mail |
| `secret_key` | Gmail app password (not your regular account password) |

To install Lambda dependencies locally:

```bash
cd lambda && npm install
```

## Additional Notes

### Nodemailer / Gmail setup

To properly set up for nodemailer:

1. Add 2 factor authentication to the Gmail account.
2. Generate and use a new app password at [https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords](https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords).

### CloudFront cache invalidation

If the site won't update in the browser despite uploading a change, the CloudFront cache likely needs to be invalidated. The `npm run deploy` script handles this automatically via `deploy:invalidate`. If you sync to S3 manually (without running the full deploy script), run `npm run deploy:invalidate` afterward.

